WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.470
- [Adam] I said that's the
problem with statistics

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.970
is that they can play into, if
they're not framed just right

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.960
they can play into the more fearful parts.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.755
- [Mike] That's exactly
what you're doing Adam!

00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:13.005
(intense violin music)

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.220
I've watched Adam Ruins
Everything in the past.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.090
Sometimes I agree, but sometimes
I very, very much disagree.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.440
So let's take a look at
his episode on hospitals.

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:25.273
Pee-whoop!

00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:26.758
- [Patient] I just need to be healthy.

00:00:26.758 --> 00:00:28.430
Just give me everything.

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.360
- Actually when it comes to medicine,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.070
getting everything could
make you less healthy.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.260
- That is a 100% accurate,
getting more care isn't

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.010
necessarily better than getting less care.

00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.897
Not only can it be a waste of money,

00:00:40.897 --> 00:00:43.130
but it can also lead to over-diagnosis,

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.740
which means giving you a
diagnosis that had you not

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:47.730
known about it, you'd live
a healthy normal life.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:50.669
Or it can lead to further
downstream testing

00:00:50.669 --> 00:00:53.090
which then can carry
their own side effects.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.560
If I give you an unnecessary test to check

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.940
for prostate cancer and
then it says you may have

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.011
prostate cancer, gives
you a false positive,

00:01:02.011 --> 00:01:05.530
then you end up getting a
prostate cancer biopsy which

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:07.902
is an invasive procedure
with a needle down there.

00:01:07.902 --> 00:01:10.590
And then as a result of
that you get an infection,

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.500
and then we find out you didn't
really have prostate cancer.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:14.820
You'd be pretty mad at me right?

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.760
- I know a hospital is expensive but

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.800
it is worth it if I
get the best treatment.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.673
- No it isn't, American
healthcare is not the best

00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:24.270
in the world, but despite
that we spend more per

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:25.103
person annually on healthcare than

00:01:25.103 --> 00:01:27.652
any other developed nation.

00:01:27.652 --> 00:01:30.410
And a big part of the reason
for that is that American

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:33.210
hospitals overcharge patients massively.

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.100
- I've seen this show before
and I really like how he

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.820
takes apart the concepts and
makes it easy to understand,

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.030
relatable and also it's very data driven.

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:44.040
However, when you take such
a complicated topic like

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:45.890
healthcare and you try and water

00:01:45.890 --> 00:01:47.330
it down to a single statement,

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.830
you're often gonna be misleading people.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.230
America's healthcare system
is not the best in the world,

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.208
and it's because hospitals
are overcharging you,

00:01:54.208 --> 00:01:57.720
that's a drastic, drastic
oversimplification.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.100
Do hospitals overcharge, yes.

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.025
The reasons they overcharge
are very important to discuss

00:02:03.025 --> 00:02:06.760
because if you just allow the
regular person to hear that,

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.440
they think hospitals are just trying

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.114
to get rich off people getting sick.

00:02:10.114 --> 00:02:12.300
It's more complex than that.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.410
- The problem starts with
something called the Chargemaster.

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:19.361
The Chargemaster is a secret
document full of insane

00:02:19.361 --> 00:02:23.580
prices that hospitals use to
charge us whatever they want.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:25.708
- Seven dollars for a single alcohol swab?

00:02:25.708 --> 00:02:27.160
That's ridiculous!

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.420
- And true.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.992
- Well, I only pay my premium.

00:02:29.992 --> 00:02:32.300
If they wanna rip off my
insurance company with their

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.010
fake prices what do I care?

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:36.240
- Insurance companies have
been paying more and more

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:37.760
out of pocket 'cause the hospitals have

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.440
been inflating their prices.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.230
The way insurance companies
have dealt with this

00:02:41.230 --> 00:02:44.380
is they try to keep your
premiums from rising too much.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.944
So you don't notice
that you're paying more,

00:02:45.944 --> 00:02:48.960
but what they do is they make
your deductible much higher,

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.360
which means that before
your insurance even kicks in

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.460
you have to pay higher amounts of money.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:54.690
- Don't have insurance?

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.184
You actually get charged
these fake prices.

00:02:58.184 --> 00:03:01.660
- [Game show Host] Let's see heart X-rays.

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:03.690
That'll be 33,000 dollars.

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.400
- This part is interestingly true.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.710
If you don't have insurance
and you have to pay out of

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.180
pocket you're gonna be
paying insane prices.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.202
A lot of hospitals, 'cause
I don't wanna make hospitals

00:03:13.202 --> 00:03:15.660
to sound like totally evil corporations,

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:17.120
they have charity care programs.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:21.030
Like my hospital, where they
actually subsidize your care.

00:03:21.030 --> 00:03:23.210
And if you fall below the poverty line,

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:27.340
you can get care extremely low
cost or even free of charge.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.622
This is why I say that it's such

00:03:28.622 --> 00:03:31.430
a dense and complicated topic.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.700
Just narrowing down and
saying hospitals are evil,

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.600
mmm...not exactly the case.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:37.870
They're not just upping the
prices for their own good.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.470
- [Doctor] Every hospital
has its own Chargemaster.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.520
A treatment that costs $7000 at one

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.951
hospital could cost 100 grand down road.

00:03:44.951 --> 00:03:47.400
- And you can't comparison
shop when you're dying.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:49.040
- So this is 100 percent true.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:50.762
When I talk about the cost of healthcare

00:03:50.762 --> 00:03:53.148
or even the cost of drug
prices like insulin,

00:03:53.148 --> 00:03:56.260
I have to make the statement
that you can't treat

00:03:56.260 --> 00:03:58.900
healthcare like you do
any other commodity.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.760
If a toy is too expensive and

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.730
people refuse to pay that cost,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:03.880
the cost of that toy is gonna go down

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.050
or the toy is gonna go off the market.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:08.530
However, if insulin is too expensive

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.120
and you need insulin to live,

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.810
whatever cost is set for
you you're gonna pay it.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.030
Even if it means going
bankrupt in the process

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.740
because you need that insulin to live.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.350
And the thing that really bothers me,

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.110
there's no transparency here.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.800
You don't know what the pharmaceutical

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.550
companies are charging,
you don't know what PBMs

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.160
which are Pharmacy Benefit Managers

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.480
are making off medications.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.100
Without the transparency, you can't make

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:32.010
an educated consumer decision.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:34.172
For example if you look
at an industry where

00:04:34.172 --> 00:04:37.950
the prices are transparent
like let's say plastic surgery.

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.270
If you go to one office
they'll tell you what it costs

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.400
to get a procedure done then
you can go to another office

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.686
and they tell you what the procedure is.

00:04:43.686 --> 00:04:46.240
The reason they do that is
that is 'cause you pay cash.

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:47.770
So they're forced to tell you.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:49.210
But when you go through insurance,

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.160
they're not necessarily
forced to tell you.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:52.630
Therefore it becomes very cloudy

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.220
and you can't make a good decision.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.310
This is why prices in the
plastic surgery industry

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.940
are much better regulated and
are much more transparent.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.960
Because they're forced to be,
because it's consumer driven.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.200
In healthcare because
you don't have a choice,

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.350
and because it's going
through the insurance company,

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.650
and there's no transparency,
the whole system falls apart.

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:13.740
- How can I stop it, what do I do?

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:17.380
- Honestly, nothing, we need
to go to the hospital so

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:19.617
they have no incentive to
change how they do business.

00:05:19.617 --> 00:05:22.501
And politicians have spent
decades arguing over how

00:05:22.501 --> 00:05:26.570
to pay the bill instead of
asking why the bill is so high.

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:28.320
- Hospitals need to exist and they

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.910
also need to turn a
profit in order to exist.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:32.766
If hospitals just start
handing out free care

00:05:32.766 --> 00:05:35.330
or not following up on the bills,

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:38.300
or taking lower premiums
from insurance companies.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:39.560
They're gonna go out of
business and we're gonna

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:41.210
have no place to get healthcare.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.951
So villainizing the hospital
system is not ideal.

00:05:43.951 --> 00:05:46.760
Working the entire
system and evaluating the

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:49.567
system as a whole and saying
the system is failing us,

00:05:49.567 --> 00:05:51.233
that's more of the big picture

00:05:51.233 --> 00:05:52.840
that we need to be looking at.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.490
Saying that politicians
have been arguing at how to

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.651
pay the bill instead of
actually fixing the problem,

00:05:57.651 --> 00:05:59.516
is a 100 percent accurate.

00:05:59.516 --> 00:06:02.598
It's so much easier as a
politician to get people behind you

00:06:02.598 --> 00:06:05.246
to say we should just pay all your bills,

00:06:05.246 --> 00:06:08.120
than actually figuring out
why the bills are so high,

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.500
and getting into the nitty
gritty details of them all.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.310
- That's it, I'll get a mammogram.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:16.610
It won't cure my cold but
it will tell me I don't have

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:19.280
cancer so at least I will
have beaten one disease today.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:21.540
I don't understand, I thought every woman

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:23.860
needed screenings like constantly.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.850
- Yeah, that's what doctors and advocacy

00:06:25.850 --> 00:06:27.900
groups have told us for decades.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.970
In the 80's, the American
Cancer Society even

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.290
ran an ad that said if you
haven't had a mammogram,

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:35.520
you need more than your breasts examined.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.350
- He wants to make it a very visceral

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:41.104
primitive reaction to
what happened in the 80's.

00:06:41.104 --> 00:06:44.280
But in the 80's the research
that we had at the time,

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.570
the best research that
we had at the time showed

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:49.920
that by getting mammograms we
can extend life and save lives

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:52.384
and prevent people from
developing worse breast cancer.

00:06:52.384 --> 00:06:57.110
Now, because we did that
research and we over screened

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.797
people unfortunately part of the course

00:06:58.797 --> 00:07:01.110
when you're doing preliminary research.

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.438
We've realized that we
should push the start date

00:07:03.438 --> 00:07:05.423
of when you should be
getting your mammograms

00:07:05.423 --> 00:07:07.350
to a little bit later in life,

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:09.543
unless you fall into
a very specific set of

00:07:09.543 --> 00:07:12.970
circumstances like if
you have a specific gene

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:15.330
mutation or family history,
that sort of thing.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.939
- Based on randomized clinical trials,

00:07:16.939 --> 00:07:19.081
if you took 10,000 women in their forties.

00:07:19.081 --> 00:07:21.660
Here's how many might die
of breast cancer if they

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:24.410
didn't get any mammograms for a decade.

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.700
And here's how many of
those same women might die

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:29.079
if they did get those
mammogram screenings.

00:07:29.079 --> 00:07:32.263
- Take off your blindfold,
they both look the same.

00:07:32.263 --> 00:07:33.630
- Exactly.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:36.250
- Very oversimplified of how
he's presenting the data.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:37.500
This research that he's talking about is

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.170
about people that died that decade.

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.960
Not taking into
consideration how that breast

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.127
cancer effected them in
their quality of life,

00:07:43.127 --> 00:07:45.290
in the decades moving forward.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:47.710
But that is the reason
in general why we moved

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.625
our recommendations to getting
screenings later in life,

00:07:51.625 --> 00:07:53.930
and getting them less frequently.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.920
Now it doesn't mean you
shouldn't get mammograms,

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.840
you absolutely should get mammograms.

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:00.350
It's just about when you
get them and that's why

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:02.527
I recommend everyone
at home to go and talk

00:08:02.527 --> 00:08:05.282
to their doctor about
what's right for them.

00:08:05.282 --> 00:08:07.310
- Mammograms find cancer.

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:09.816
I have many pamphlets that say that.

00:08:09.816 --> 00:08:12.370
- [Female Doctor] Well they
definitely find cancer,

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.160
but not all cancer is the same.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.070
- [Adam] There's the kind of
cancer you were thinking of,

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:17.690
the kind you hope to catch early.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.480
- Yeah, that one hasn't
reached the finish line yet.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.060
A mammogram could stop it in time.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:22.893
- It might.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.260
The problem is mammograms
can't tell the difference

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:29.100
between these types of cancer.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.790
- That's true but that's why
when you get a mammogram,

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.220
we don't just do a mammogram
and then say oh well you

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:35.640
either have cancer or you don't.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:39.010
We follow that up with a
biopsy which then tells us

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.750
which type of cancer it is and that's

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:41.930
how we guide our treatment.

00:08:41.930 --> 00:08:44.880
So again, an oversimplification that

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:46.580
can confuse the average person.

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.010
- [Female Doctor] There's a
chance the X-ray will show

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.380
something suspicious when the

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:51.230
woman doesn't even have cancer.

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:52.931
That's called a false positive.

00:08:52.931 --> 00:08:54.810
This happens to about one out of ten

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:56.920
women and it can be very stressful.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:58.810
- [Adam] And the more
often you get mammograms,

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.390
the more likely you are
to get a false positive.

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:04.558
- I try and watch this from
an average person perspective.

00:09:04.558 --> 00:09:08.170
And when I do that, what
I feel is mammograms

00:09:08.170 --> 00:09:09.990
are useless and can actually hurt me.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.001
That's not the message
we wanna send to people.

00:09:12.001 --> 00:09:14.382
The message we wanna send to people is go

00:09:14.382 --> 00:09:17.100
talk to your doctor and
if you need a mammogram

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:19.100
your doctor recommends
them, it's a good thing.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.718
Now challenge them and ask
them why or should you wait,

00:09:21.718 --> 00:09:23.720
and ask good questions.

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:26.200
But putting the information
out to the general public

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:28.710
like this that doesn't have a
lot of scientific knowledge?

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.450
You can really confuse people and actually

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.120
encourage them to not get screened.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:35.150
Most people will do
anything to skip out on

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.530
one of these cancer screenings.

00:09:36.530 --> 00:09:38.870
And to have a show like
this tell them that it's

00:09:38.870 --> 00:09:41.320
not the greatest test in the world?

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:44.150
A woman who's 55 will
watch this and who already

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:46.600
has a predisposition to
not wanna get a screening

00:09:46.600 --> 00:09:48.318
will watch this and say
I'm just not gonna get it,

00:09:48.318 --> 00:09:50.420
and that's not what we want to happen.

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:53.010
- If you do feel a lump
or an abnormality please

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.390
go see your doctor and if they recommend

00:09:55.390 --> 00:09:57.040
a mammogram, get one at that time.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:58.384
- Exactly that's good advice,

00:09:58.384 --> 00:10:00.700
but the problem is all
that advice comes after

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.330
you just scared the
bejeezes out of people who

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:04.900
might actually need mammograms.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:08.433
So I'm not a fan of the way
that this mammogram special

00:10:08.433 --> 00:10:11.560
was presented, even
though factually accurate.

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.110
- There is some good news,
our treatment of breast

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:16.640
cancer is so much better
than it used to be.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:18.930
The mortality has dropped significantly.

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:22.010
- The reason why our
treatment is so much better

00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:24.160
is because we found so many of

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:26.500
these cancers early because of mammograms.

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:28.820
They spent all this time
knocking mammograms,

00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:31.870
at least give credit to
mammograms for causing

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.631
breast cancer mortality rates to drop.

00:10:33.631 --> 00:10:36.090
- [Female Doctor] One
in eight in a lifetime,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.990
and it's of a diagnosis not of death.

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:39.911
- I see, I see, that's the
problem with statistic is that

00:10:39.911 --> 00:10:42.370
they can play into, if
they're not framed just right

00:10:42.370 --> 00:10:44.557
they can play into the more fearful parts.

00:10:44.557 --> 00:10:47.040
- That's exactly what you're doing Adam.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.845
I mean yes, we don't say
one in eight women will die

00:10:49.845 --> 00:10:52.380
from breast cancer we
say they'll develop it.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:54.590
So if you're being clear with
the statistic that you're

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.727
giving, a patient or a person
can make their own decision on

00:10:57.727 --> 00:10:59.640
how they feel about that statistic.

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.470
Adam has great intentions
and he wants to present

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:04.021
information, he wants it to
be controversial and edgy

00:11:04.021 --> 00:11:05.866
and disruptive, and that's good.

00:11:05.866 --> 00:11:09.150
But unless you've had
clinical experience where you

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.026
understand how patients normally come in.

00:11:11.026 --> 00:11:13.230
You won't be able to tailor that

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:14.979
information to do something meaningful.

00:11:14.979 --> 00:11:17.240
Like if I was to make this show,

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:19.526
I would make it with the
presumption and the knowledge

00:11:19.526 --> 00:11:22.498
that patients come in
generally afraid of mammograms.

00:11:22.498 --> 00:11:25.285
And I would be able to tailor
the show with that in mind.

00:11:25.285 --> 00:11:29.180
And maybe it won't be as
funny or edgy or disruptive,

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.049
but at least it'll be meaningful.

00:11:31.049 --> 00:11:34.310
'Cause that's the idea of
making quality health content.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:37.560
It's to not only entertain
but to somehow give

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.322
a piece of education that's
gonna make a lasting impact.

00:11:41.322 --> 00:11:44.690
And that's why have to
always measure the intentions

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:46.830
and then see if the outcomes
match the intentions.

00:11:46.830 --> 00:11:50.290
'Cause I bet if I show this to
a 100 women who are let's say

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.810
at the age let's say 50
years old to get a mammogram.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.810
And I let them watch this really quickly,

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:57.180
and ask them what they feel
about mammograms after.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:12:00.080
I guarantee you the majority
of them will be more turned off

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:01.831
on mammograms even
though they shouldn't be,

00:12:01.831 --> 00:12:04.680
because of this show, and
that's a negative outcome.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:08.258
Did you see where Dr. Mike
ruins Detox Teas, click here.

00:12:08.258 --> 00:12:10.333
And stay happy and healthy.

00:12:10.333 --> 00:12:13.750
(chill electronic music)

